If you want, I can also:
- switch this to **plain HTML/JS** (no React) while keeping the same roving-focus behavior, or
- add **edit-in-place on Enter**, and a proper “don’t steal focus when clicking checkboxes” refinement.

A few important nits + a handful of “v1 will work, but you’ll feel it quickly” improvements. None of these change the overall structure—you’re very close.

## 1) Date keys: `toISOString()` uses UTC (can mis-group “days”)
Right now:

```ts
const dayKey = (ms: number) => new Date(ms).toISOString().slice(0, 10);
const todayKey = () => new Date().toISOString().slice(0,10);
```

That groups by **UTC day**, not the user’s local day. Around midnight, items will land in “yesterday/tomorrow” unexpectedly for many timezones.

If you mean “created day in the user’s locale”, use a local day key:

```ts
export function localDayKey(ms: number) {
  const d = new Date(ms);
  const yyyy = d.getFullYear();
  const mm = String(d.getMonth() + 1).padStart(2, "0");
  const dd = String(d.getDate()).padStart(2, "0");
  return `${yyyy}-${mm}-${dd}`;
}
```

Then swap usages in `derive.ts` and `todayKey`.

## 2) `now` and `tKey` recompute every render (causes unnecessary churn)
In `App.tsx`:

```ts
const tKey = todayKey();
const now = Date.now();
```

Because these are computed on every render, your derived lists can reshuffle even when nothing changed (especially `recentIds`).

Minimal fix: compute them once per mount and update on a timer (or update only when needed).

Example: update “now” every minute and “today” when the day flips:

```ts
const [now, setNow] = useState(() => Date.now());
useEffect(() => {
  const id = window.setInterval(() => setNow(Date.now()), 60_000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);

const [tKey, setTKey] = useState(() => localDayKey(Date.now()));
useEffect(() => {
  const id = window.setInterval(() => {
    const next = localDayKey(Date.now());
    setTKey(prev => (prev === next ? prev : next));
  }, 60_000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

This keeps recents “rolling” without re-deriving on every keystroke elsewhere.

## 3) Global keys: `Escape` should not clear capture unless capture is focused
Your current global handler:

```ts
if (opts.hasCaptureText()) {
  opts.clearCapture();
  return;
}
```

This will clear the capture text even if the user is typing in some future editor field (or focused in Today rows) as long as capture has text.

Change the rule to: clear capture **only if capture input is the active element**.

```ts
const isCaptureFocused = () =>
  document.activeElement === opts.captureRef.current;

if (key === "escape") {
  if (isCaptureFocused() && opts.hasCaptureText()) {
    e.preventDefault();
    opts.clearCapture();
    return;
  }
  if (isTextInput(e.target)) return;
  opts.collapsePanels();
}
```

## 4) `useGlobalKeys` is re-attaching the listener every render
Because you pass an object literal into `useGlobalKeys({ ... })`, the `opts` identity changes each render, so your `useEffect(...,[opts])` runs each time.

Two easy fixes:

### Option A (simplest): depend on stable primitives/callbacks
In `App.tsx`, wrap handlers in `useCallback`, and pass them individually, not as an object.

### Option B: keep object but don’t use `[opts]` as a dependency
If you keep the object shape, store it in a ref internally:

```ts
const optsRef = useRef(opts);
optsRef.current = opts;

useEffect(() => {
  const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent) => { /* use optsRef.current */ };
  window.addEventListener("keydown", onKeyDown);
  return () => window.removeEventListener("keydown", onKeyDown);
}, []);
```

Either way avoids re-registering global listeners.

## 5) RecentPanel: `flatIds.indexOf(id)` inside render is O(n²)
This line:

```ts
const globalIndex = flatIds.indexOf(id);
```

Inside nested maps will get slow quickly. Precompute an index map once:

```ts
const indexById = useMemo(() => {
  const m = new Map<string, number>();
  flatIds.forEach((id, i) => m.set(id, i));
  return m;
}, [flatIds]);

// then:
const globalIndex = indexById.get(id)!;
```

## 6) Roving focus + clicking controls: you currently block keyboard access to the checkbox/button
You set `tabIndex={-1}` on checkbox/button to keep roving focus on the row. That’s fine, but then:
- mouse users can toggle the checkbox
- keyboard users must use Space on the row (your handler)
- screen reader users may prefer actual focusable controls

A common refinement is:
- keep roving focus on the row for arrow navigation
- but allow Enter to “activate” the primary control, and keep the checkbox focusable when navigating by Tab (or provide an “Actions” button)

If you want to keep your approach, consider adding `aria-checked` on the row and set `role="checkbox"` for tasks (or `role="button"`), so assistive tech gets a clearer state model. Right now the row is just a generic `listitem`.

Minimal accessibility upgrade for the row:
- make the row `role="checkbox"` and keep it in a list (or use `role="option"` in a `listbox`)
- set `aria-checked={!!completedAt}`

## 7) Undo: you set `expiresAt` but never auto-expire it
You do check expiry in `undoDelete`, but the toast remains indefinitely unless dismissed. Add an effect:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  if (!lastDeleted) return;
  const ms = Math.max(0, lastDeleted.expiresAt - Date.now());
  const id = window.setTimeout(() => setLastDeleted(null), ms);
  return () => clearTimeout(id);
}, [lastDeleted]);
```

## 8) Small logic concerns / simplifications
- `deriveTodayIds`: `pinnedToToday || dueDate === todayKey` is ok, but in your add flow you always set `pinnedToToday: true`, so due dates won’t matter unless you later update items. That’s fine for v1, just be aware “Today” will become “all open tasks” unless you later unpin.
- `toggleComplete`: you don’t clear `pinnedToToday` when completing. If you later want “Today = pinned open tasks”, you’re okay; if you want completion to remove from Today implicitly, you already filter `!completedAt`, so fine.

